LICENSES FOR if_bcm4313 (FreeBSD Broadcom BCM4313 802.11n driver)
================================================================

Files described by sections 1-3 are dual-licensed BSD-2-Clause OR
GPL-2.0-or-later as a single coherent rewrite (see section 1).  The firmware
in section 5 is NOT dual-licensed -- it remains under Broadcom's agreement.
COPYING holds the GPL-2.0 text used when the GPL option is exercised.

This project is a personal, out-of-tree FreeBSD kernel module.  It
intentionally does NOT isolate licensing -- it reuses the hardware register
map and tuning data of the Linux `brcmsmac` driver plus the Broadcom D11/LCN
firmware blobs directly.  Make sure you are comfortable with the terms below
before distributing anything built from this tree.

The module is a CASCADE of differently-licensed components.  Each part is
licensed under its own terms; the obligations of all of them apply to the
combined module:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Original driver glue -- if_bcm4313.c / if_bcm4313var.h / Makefile
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Written for this project against FreeBSD's native interfaces (net80211,
bus_dma(9), bus_space(9), bhnd(4)).  DUAL-LICENSED:

  SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause OR GPL-2.0-or-later

This means redistributors may choose, per binary/source release, to use the
BSD-2-Clause terms (below) OR the GPL-2.0-or-later terms for the driver code.
Both are compatible with the ISC-licensed tuning data (section 3).  There is
no linuxkpi and no GPL-only symbol linkage on the FreeBSD side, so a BSD build
stays clean for FreeBSD/ports, while a GPL build is available if you ever want
to fold it into a GPL-licensed kernel tree.  BSD option -- BSD-2-Clause terms:

  Redistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without
  modification, are permitted provided that the following conditions are met:

  1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright notice,
     this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.
  2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright notice,
     this list of conditions and the following disclaimer in the documentation
     and/or other materials provided with the distribution.

  THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS "AS IS"
  AND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, THE
  IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE
  ARE DISCLAIMED. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS OR CONTRIBUTORS BE
  LIABLE FOR ANY DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY, OR
  CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES (INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, PROCUREMENT OF
  SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR SERVICES; LOSS OF USE, DATA, OR PROFITS; OR BUSINESS
  INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER CAUSED AND ON ANY THEORY OF LIABILITY, WHETHER IN
  CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR TORT (INCLUDING NEGLIGENCE OR OTHERWISE)
  ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE OF THIS SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE
  POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.

  ALTERNATIVE OPTION -- GPL-2.0-or-later: if you choose the GNU General Public
  License, version 2 or any later version, for the driver code instead of
  BSD-2-Clause, you may do so; the full GPL-2.0 text is provided in the file
  COPYING and must accompany any GPL-licensed distribution.  The kernel itself
  is not re-licensed; only your chosen use of this driver component.

  WHY BOTH OPTIONS ARE AVAILABLE: this is a full rewrite, but its register map
  and design are translated from the GPL-2.0 Linux kernel brcmsmac driver.
  Dual-licensing BSD-2-Clause OR GPL-2.0-or-later is what makes the rewrite
  clean to reuse on either side -- a BSD consumer takes the BSD option, and
  anyone linking into a GPL-licensed kernel tree takes (or must match) the
  GPL option, which fully accounts for the GPL provenance.

  IMPORTANT -- the ISC license section 3 allows this ISC data to be carried in
  either a BSD or GPL build (ISC is compatible with both), but it stays ISC.
  It cannot be independently re-licensed by you, only sublicensed within the
two options above.

  NOTE ON PROVENANCE: the register/constant values and the driver design are
  translated from the Linux kernel `brcmsmac` driver
  (drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/), which lives in the
  GPL-2.0 Linux kernel.  The individual brcmsmac files carry a per-file ISC
  grant from Broadcom (see section 3 below), which underpins the BSD reuse;
  and because this is a full rewrite, choosing the GPL-2.0-or-later option
  fully accounts for the GPL-kernel provenance in any GPL-linked context.  If
  you distribute under BSD only, the ISC grant is what keeps the translated
  data usable, so those terms must be preserved.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. FreeBSD derivation, attribution and trademark -- READ THIS ONE TOO
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The driver targets FreeBSD and follows FreeBSD kernel conventions
($FreeBSD$ keyword, bus_dma(9)/bus_space(9)/net80211/bhnd(4) patterns, and
the kmod build via bsd.kmod.mk).  Its structure and several API call patterns
were modeled on FreeBSD kernel drivers such as bwn(4).  FreeBSD itself, and
the kernel interfaces used here, are distributed under the FreeBSD License
(BSD-2-Clause); the FreeBSD project's copyright and license terms therefore
apply to the portions derived from it.

  FreeBSD - Copyright (c) The FreeBSD Project.  All rights reserved.
  SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause (see section 1 for the text).

Redistributions of source and binary forms based on FreeBSD code must retain
this copyright notice in the source and in the binary's accompanying
materials; the driver and this project do so.  When shipping binary kernels
or modules built from FreeBSD-derived code, you must reproduce the FreeBSD
license text (section 1) in the distribution.

TRADEMARK: "FreeBSD" is a registered trademark of the FreeBSD Foundation.
This is an independent, unofficial driver and is in no way affiliated with or
endorsed by the FreeBSD Foundation, and it must not be presented as an official
FreeBSD deliverable or as endorsed by the project.  Non-remote references to
"FreeBSD" here are nominative/descriptive (the OS it runs on).  If you fork,
rename, or repackage this work, do not imply FreeBSD Foundation endorsement.
Check https://www.freebsdfoundation.org for the project's trademark and
logo-usage guidelines before any public distribution under the FreeBSD name.

